chief exec  of network rail is uh with us how's it  looking  well it's devastating for passengers i  mean this is a this is a wasteland it's  like the darkest days of covert  passengers alienated from the railway  because we can't run a service for them  and uh  breaks my heart it's  really really apologize to passengers  who are facing that and we know there's  some real life  issues for people who can't travel today  it's just it's so wrong who's running  the trains that are running  uh  well the trains are being run by train  driver because they're not on strike but  we have um managers who are fully  qualified to be signalers and things and  who are running the signal boxes  uh but that's what we've only got a  limited number of them which is why  we've got reduced hours of operation and  why some routes are not open how much  have you offered um rmt uh um i'm not  going to negotiate with the rmt on you  know  others are saying three percent neither  of those figures are correct we've been  clear  than that it is a lot more than that  because what we've said is  there's real inefficiency in britain's  railways we can be more productive we  can save the taxpayer money and we can  do a better pay rise for colleagues in  the role as well and that's what we want  to do we want to reform working  practices that are holding the railway  back  and that means we can unlock then a  better pay rise for the workers they  want seven percent  like i said i'm not going to get into  specific numbers so however however many  times you ask me but are you anywhere  near that though  look we can there's real it depends how  much reform we get that's the point i  think there's really huge reforms that  we can do to work and practice we've got  some crazy outmoded practices  that drive inefficiency that make the  railway less safe actually make it  harder for people to work here  but um you know  we've we've had a real resistance for  making those reforms we want to make  those happen we want to talk to the rmt  and get the railway running again mr  sharps the transport secretary said he  would it would make matters worse if he  was in the room for negotiations do you  agree with him oh i do look i've worked  in the private sector and you'd never  have the shareholder in the room it's up  to management to talk to the trade union  yes the government sets a framework but  they've given me enough room to  negotiate it's the fact that the rmt  doesn't want to facilitate the reform  that's the issue here  how would you describe the mood of the  rmt when they're negotiating with you at  the moment  i would say generally good but just  every now and again it's as if  you know something belligerent takes  over them i mean we've had hugely  constructive conversations we think  we're getting there  on sunday evening eight o'clock we  thought we were literally within a  hair's breath of a deal by half past  eight they were walking away same thing  again yesterday it feels as if  uh you know we get them almost to the  line  and then they fall back when are you  going to start talking again  well hopefully tomorrow i think today  everybody is busy concentrating on  running a railway doing the very best  job for the few passengers who are  traveling  but like i want to talk to them we will  talk to them day and night 24 7 to try  to get the next strikes called off do  you think the strikes as as we speak now  are likely to go ahead on thursday and  saturday i think they're likely and i  think people should plan on that basis  but i will do everything i can to try  and turn that round but i can't i can't  be confident about that at this stage  what would you say to the rmt union if  they're listening right now  talk we've got real reforms which can  give a generous pay award  to my colleagues in network rail  that can actually be good news for  taxpayers for passengers and those  colleagues i don't think those  colleagues want to lose money i think  few of them can afford it we all know  what inflation is doing we know what the  cost of living is in britain at the  moment they don't want to be losing pay  we need to be talking  to get this fixed the government says  that if these strikes continue to go  ahead they're going to change the law so  that agency workers can do the jobs that  rmt members are doing at the moment with  that is that something you'd support  well it doesn't have a huge impact on us  uh because when you become a s  to be a signal or to be trained as a  contingent signal it's a huge amount of  preparation obviously because we don't  compromise our safety standards when  iota so it's not really practical but i  think in other parts of the industry and  other parts of the economy the use of  agency workers i know would make a big  difference certainly in my previous  experience in aviation it would help i  know other parts of the economy and i  think it's inevitable uh when we see  such intransigence over fundamental  reforms how frustrated are you  i think you can get the sense i'm  massively frustrated because this is a  wasteland it should be teaming with  passengers going about their daily lives  and as a system we're letting them down  and we can't carry on like that thursday  and saturday um if there is no  resolution will you be able to run a  similar level of service it's a sim yeah  it's effectively it's the same as today  uh for those uh future strike dates  tomorrow sadly disrupted because the  people who should be coming or not night  term to set the railway up for tomorrow  morning  will uh have been instructed by the mt  not to do that so tomorrow morning will  be really damaged we hope by late  morning a normal service running to the  end of the day but disruption as well on  wednesday friday and sunday and that's  why this is so so damaging okay good to  talk to you thank you so much for taking  the time

